{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-8718" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\sqrt{3}$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981396/state.json" "file_sha256" "26ae3c9abe0d08d8ded9442eb5263794a2fefbe79397a2745b5a2d5194c4ff76"} {"question" "q-8718" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}=\\pi +{x}_{1}=\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3}=-\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981396/state.json" "file_sha256" "26ae3c9abe0d08d8ded9442eb5263794a2fefbe79397a2745b5a2d5194c4ff76"} {"question" "q-8718" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981396/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b665e54559a31834b41dae8fc15cbb345777229ebe367bf02150bee5b0b6b70e"} {"question" "q-8718" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981396/state.json" "file_sha256" "26ae3c9abe0d08d8ded9442eb5263794a2fefbe79397a2745b5a2d5194c4ff76"} {"question" "q-8718" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}"] ["math" "-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}"] ["math" "-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}"] ["math" "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{36}"] ["math" "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981396/state.json" "file_sha256" "26ae3c9abe0d08d8ded9442eb5263794a2fefbe79397a2745b5a2d5194c4ff76"} {"question" "q-8718" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981396/state.json" "file_sha256" "26ae3c9abe0d08d8ded9442eb5263794a2fefbe79397a2745b5a2d5194c4ff76"} {"question" "q-8723" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions, in radians, of the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-2$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$ what is the value to $3$ decimal places of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981396/state.json" "file_sha256" "26ae3c9abe0d08d8ded9442eb5263794a2fefbe79397a2745b5a2d5194c4ff76"} {"question" "q-8723" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-2)=-1.107\\,148...≈-1.107\\,149\n$$\n\nSince $-1.107\\,149$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-1.107\\,149.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\pi +{x}_{1}=\\pi -1.107\\,148=2.034\\,444.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-1.107\\,149,2.034\\,444.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈-1.107\\,149\\cdot 2.034\\,444≈-2.252\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981396/state.json" "file_sha256" "26ae3c9abe0d08d8ded9442eb5263794a2fefbe79397a2745b5a2d5194c4ff76"} {"question" "q-8723" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981396/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b665e54559a31834b41dae8fc15cbb345777229ebe367bf02150bee5b0b6b70e"} {"question" "q-8723" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981396/state.json" "file_sha256" "26ae3c9abe0d08d8ded9442eb5263794a2fefbe79397a2745b5a2d5194c4ff76"} {"question" "q-8723" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-0.252"] ["math" "-2.252"] ["math" "-2.448"] ["math" "-2.470"] ["math" "-3.252"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981396/state.json" "file_sha256" "26ae3c9abe0d08d8ded9442eb5263794a2fefbe79397a2745b5a2d5194c4ff76"} {"question" "q-8723" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2.252"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981396/state.json" "file_sha256" "26ae3c9abe0d08d8ded9442eb5263794a2fefbe79397a2745b5a2d5194c4ff76"} {"question" "q-72833" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981396/assets/aae559883c97bbd19321d9baeb26fe15e47cced5b072e12100c42aa579c9d02d.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and the line $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.$ Find, in radians, the $x$-coordinate of the point $A.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981396/state.json" "file_sha256" "26ae3c9abe0d08d8ded9442eb5263794a2fefbe79397a2745b5a2d5194c4ff76"} {"question" "q-72833" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The $y$-values of both functions are the same at the intersection point. Equating the two functions gives:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n$$\n\n Now, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies inside the required domain, and there is only one point of intersection, we conclude that the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point $A$ is $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981396/state.json" "file_sha256" "26ae3c9abe0d08d8ded9442eb5263794a2fefbe79397a2745b5a2d5194c4ff76"} {"question" "q-72833" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981396/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b665e54559a31834b41dae8fc15cbb345777229ebe367bf02150bee5b0b6b70e"} {"question" "q-72833" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981396/state.json" "file_sha256" "26ae3c9abe0d08d8ded9442eb5263794a2fefbe79397a2745b5a2d5194c4ff76"} {"question" "q-72833" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{3\\pi }{8}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{8}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981396/state.json" "file_sha256" "26ae3c9abe0d08d8ded9442eb5263794a2fefbe79397a2745b5a2d5194c4ff76"} {"question" "q-72833" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi }{6}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981396/state.json" "file_sha256" "26ae3c9abe0d08d8ded9442eb5263794a2fefbe79397a2745b5a2d5194c4ff76"} {"question" "q-321613" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the solutions **in radians** to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{3}{2}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ Round your answers to **one decimal place**.\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981396/state.json" "file_sha256" "26ae3c9abe0d08d8ded9442eb5263794a2fefbe79397a2745b5a2d5194c4ff76"} {"question" "q-321613" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arctan\\,(\\frac{3}{2})=0.982\\,793…≈1.0\n$$\n\nSince $1.0$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=1.0.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\pi +0.982\\,793…≈4.1.\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are $x=1.0$ and $x=4.1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981396/state.json" "file_sha256" "26ae3c9abe0d08d8ded9442eb5263794a2fefbe79397a2745b5a2d5194c4ff76"} {"question" "q-321613" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981396/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b665e54559a31834b41dae8fc15cbb345777229ebe367bf02150bee5b0b6b70e"} {"question" "q-321613" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981396/state.json" "file_sha256" "26ae3c9abe0d08d8ded9442eb5263794a2fefbe79397a2745b5a2d5194c4ff76"} {"question" "q-321613" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981396/state.json" "file_sha256" "26ae3c9abe0d08d8ded9442eb5263794a2fefbe79397a2745b5a2d5194c4ff76"} {"question" "q-321613" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981396/state.json" "file_sha256" "26ae3c9abe0d08d8ded9442eb5263794a2fefbe79397a2745b5a2d5194c4ff76"} {"question" "q-321613" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4.1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981396/state.json" "file_sha256" "26ae3c9abe0d08d8ded9442eb5263794a2fefbe79397a2745b5a2d5194c4ff76"} {"question" "q-321728" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the solutions **in degrees** to the equation $5\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+2=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ Round your answers to **the nearest integer**.\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981396/state.json" "file_sha256" "26ae3c9abe0d08d8ded9442eb5263794a2fefbe79397a2745b5a2d5194c4ff76"} {"question" "q-321728" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+2 & =0 \\\\ 5\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-2 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-0.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-0.4)=-21.8014{…}^{∘}≈-{22.80}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince $-{22.80}^{∘}$ lies outside the domain, it is not a solution.\n\nTo generate solutions in the domain, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={180}^{∘}+(-{21.80}^{∘})≈{158.20}^{∘},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={360}^{∘}+(-{21.80}^{∘})≈{338.20}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order and rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions are $x={158}^{∘}$ and $x={338}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981396/state.json" "file_sha256" "26ae3c9abe0d08d8ded9442eb5263794a2fefbe79397a2745b5a2d5194c4ff76"} {"question" "q-321728" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981396/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b665e54559a31834b41dae8fc15cbb345777229ebe367bf02150bee5b0b6b70e"} {"question" "q-321728" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981396/state.json" "file_sha256" "26ae3c9abe0d08d8ded9442eb5263794a2fefbe79397a2745b5a2d5194c4ff76"} {"question" "q-321728" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "158"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981396/state.json" "file_sha256" "26ae3c9abe0d08d8ded9442eb5263794a2fefbe79397a2745b5a2d5194c4ff76"} {"question" "q-321728" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981396/state.json" "file_sha256" "26ae3c9abe0d08d8ded9442eb5263794a2fefbe79397a2745b5a2d5194c4ff76"} {"question" "q-321728" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "338"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981396/state.json" "file_sha256" "26ae3c9abe0d08d8ded9442eb5263794a2fefbe79397a2745b5a2d5194c4ff76"}] "usage" {"q-8718" []} "basis" 887 "retractions" [[17592186092825 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nGiven that $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\tan x =-\\sqrt{3}$ for $- π < x \\le π$, what is the value of $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$?"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186091416 17592186091417 17592186091418 17592186091419 17592186091420] "immutable_fields" [[17592186091415 :answer-field/correct] [17592186091415 :answer-field/id] [17592186091415 :answer-field/key] [17592186091415 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
